BODY = """
<h3>A disease treated only when it causes problems</h3>
<p>Most patients with CLL are diagnosed without symptoms, and many never need treatment. That principle has survived every new drug. Genetic testing by FISH separated patients with a 17p deletion, whose median survival was 32 months, from those with a 13q deletion alone, at 133 months [[Genomic aberrations by FISH (Döhner)]]. The CLL-IPI added TP53 and IGHV status to stage, age and β2-microglobulin to predict survival [[CLL-IPI (International CLL-IPI working group)]], and the IPS-E predicts how soon an early-stage patient will need treatment [[IPS-E (Condoluci)]]. The iwCLL criteria define active disease, the indication to start [[iwCLL 2018 guidelines (Hallek)]]. When early treatment was tested with a modern drug, ibrutinib delayed progression in higher-risk Binet A disease but survival was the same, 93.3% vs 93.6% at 5 years [[CLL12 (Langerbeins)]].</p>

<h3>The chemoimmunotherapy era</h3>
<p>Fludarabine gave more and longer responses than chlorambucil without improving survival [[Fludarabine vs chlorambucil (Rai)]], and adding cyclophosphamide raised 5-year PFS from 10% to 36%, again without a survival gain [[LRF CLL4 (Catovsky)]]. Rituximab changed that. FCR produced complete responses in 70% of patients in its phase 2 trial [[FCR phase 2 (Keating)]], and in CLL8 it improved both PFS and overall survival over FC [[CLL8 (Hallek)]]. With long follow-up, about half of patients with mutated IGHV remained progression-free beyond 12 years, with a plateau that still argues for FCR in a few selected patients [[FCR phase 2 (Keating)]].</p>
<p>FCR was too toxic for most patients, who are older or have comorbidities. Bendamustine was better than chlorambucil [[Bendamustine vs chlorambucil (Knauf)]], and with rituximab it was less toxic than FCR but gave shorter PFS in fit patients, 41.7 vs 55.2 months [[CLL10 (Eichhorst)]]. For patients with comorbidities, obinutuzumab-chlorambucil gave a median PFS of 26.7 months and became the standard, and the comparator for the trials that followed [[CLL11 (Goede)]] [[COMPLEMENT 1 (Hillmen)]].</p>

<h3>Continuous BTK inhibitors</h3>
<p>Ibrutinib moved to first-line treatment with RESONATE-2, where it improved PFS and survival over chlorambucil; in the final analysis median PFS was 8.9 years [[RESONATE-2 (Burger)]]. It then beat each chemoimmunotherapy standard: bendamustine-rituximab in older patients, where rituximab added nothing to ibrutinib [[Alliance A041202 (Woyach)]]; chlorambucil-obinutuzumab [[iLLUMINATE (Moreno)]]; and FCR in younger patients, with better overall survival in E1912 [[E1912 (Shanafelt)]] but not in FLAIR, which also reported more sudden cardiac deaths with ibrutinib [[FLAIR: ibrutinib-rituximab (Hillmen)]].</p>
<p>The more selective inhibitors followed the same path. Acalabrutinib, with or without obinutuzumab, was better than chlorambucil-obinutuzumab, and after 6 years the combination had longer PFS than acalabrutinib alone and longer survival than chemoimmunotherapy [[ELEVATE-TN (Sharman)]]. Zanubrutinib was better than bendamustine-rituximab, with a 5-year hazard ratio for PFS of 0.29 [[SEQUOIA (Tam)]]. Pirtobrutinib, a noncovalent inhibitor, has now done the same [[BRUIN CLL-313 (Jurczak)]]. The trials that compared BTK inhibitors with each other (ELEVATE-RR, ALPINE and BRUIN CLL-314) were run mainly in relapsed disease and belong to the relapsed CLL topic; they are the reason acalabrutinib and zanubrutinib are generally preferred to ibrutinib.</p>

<h3>Fixed-duration venetoclax combinations</h3>
<p>CLL14 gave 12 cycles of venetoclax-obinutuzumab to patients with comorbidities. After 6 years, median PFS was 76.2 months against 36.4 with chlorambucil-obinutuzumab, most of it off treatment [[CLL14 (Fischer)]]. CLL13 extended the regimen to fit patients: venetoclax-obinutuzumab, with or without ibrutinib, was better than FCR or bendamustine-rituximab, while venetoclax-rituximab was not [[CLL13/GAIA (Eichhorst)]], and CRISTALLO confirmed the deeper responses [[CRISTALLO (Sharman)]].</p>
<p>Combining a BTK inhibitor with venetoclax gave an all-oral regimen. CAPTIVATE defined it [[CAPTIVATE (Wierda)]]; GLOW showed better PFS and, with long follow-up, better survival than chlorambucil-obinutuzumab in older patients [[GLOW (Kater)]]; and FLAIR, which adjusted duration to the MRD response, reported a 5-year PFS of 93.9% against 79.0% with ibrutinib alone and 58.1% with FCR [[FLAIR: ibrutinib-venetoclax (Munir)]]. Ibrutinib-venetoclax was not approved in the United States. AMPLIFY tested acalabrutinib-venetoclax for 14 cycles, with a 36-month PFS of 76.5% against 66.5% with chemoimmunotherapy, and led to the US approval of that combination in 2026 [[AMPLIFY (Brown)]].</p>

<h3>Fixed duration or continuous?</h3>
<p>CLL17 is the first trial to compare the two strategies directly. At 3 years, PFS was 81.1% with venetoclax-obinutuzumab, 79.4% with venetoclax-ibrutinib and 81.0% with continuous ibrutinib, and both fixed-duration regimens met non-inferiority [[CLL17 (Al-Sawaf)]]. The follow-up is short, and the answer may differ by subgroup: after venetoclax-obinutuzumab, remissions are shorter with del(17p) and unmutated IGHV [[CLL14 (Fischer)]], while with continuous ibrutinib PFS was not inferior in patients with TP53 abnormalities [[Alliance A041202 (Woyach)]].</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>This is a personal summary, not a guideline; NCCN placements were not checked against the current document.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Asymptomatic early stage:</b> observation, whatever the risk factors [[CLL12 (Langerbeins)]].</li>
<li><b>Before treatment:</b> FISH for del(17p), TP53 sequencing and IGHV mutation status [[iwCLL 2018 guidelines (Hallek)]].</li>
<li><b>Needs treatment, no TP53 aberration:</b> a choice between a continuous BTK inhibitor (acalabrutinib, zanubrutinib or pirtobrutinib) and a fixed-duration regimen (venetoclax-obinutuzumab or acalabrutinib-venetoclax), by comorbidities, IGHV status, logistics and preference.</li>
<li><b>del(17p) or TP53 mutation:</b> a continuous BTK inhibitor is the usual choice; most fixed-duration trials excluded these patients.</li>
<li><b>Chemoimmunotherapy:</b> FCR only for selected young, fit patients with IGHV-mutated disease and no TP53 aberration who want a single short treatment.</li>
<li><b>Maintenance and transplant:</b> not part of first-line treatment today. Allogeneic transplant is reserved for relapsed high-risk disease and Richter transformation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed duration or continuous therapy:</b> longer follow-up of CLL17, by IGHV and TP53 status, and how well each strategy can be repeated at relapse.</li>
<li><b>Which fixed-duration regimen:</b> venetoclax with obinutuzumab or with a BTK inhibitor, and whether triplets are worth their toxicity [[CLL13/GAIA (Eichhorst)]] [[AMPLIFY (Brown)]].</li>
<li><b>MRD-guided duration:</b> effective in FLAIR, but not yet tested with the newer BTK inhibitors.</li>
<li><b>TP53-aberrant disease:</b> still the group with the shortest remissions and the least randomized evidence. A nonrandomized cohort of zanubrutinib-venetoclax enrolled 66 patients with TP53 aberration among 114, with an estimated 24-month PFS of 92% [[SEQUOIA arm D (Shadman)]]; it has no randomized comparator.</li>
<li><b>Early treatment:</b> whether a fixed-duration regimen in the highest-risk asymptomatic patients improves survival.</li>
<li><b>Richter transformation:</b> not covered here; it will be a separate section built later.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>Ongoing phase 3 trials (listed under Trials to watch) compare acalabrutinib-venetoclax with venetoclax-obinutuzumab (MAJIC), test sonrotoclax with zanubrutinib against venetoclax-obinutuzumab, against venetoclax-acalabrutinib and against continuous zanubrutinib, and ask whether venetoclax added to ibrutinib-obinutuzumab helps (A041702, EA9161). CLL16 tests a triplet in high-risk disease, EVOLVE CLL/SLL tests early venetoclax-obinutuzumab in high-risk asymptomatic patients, BELLWAVE-011 compares nemtabrutinib with covalent BTK inhibitors, and SOUNDTRACK-C1 tests consolidation with a bispecific antibody. BTK degraders are in phase 3 in relapsed disease (see New drugs to watch).</p>
"""
